-- PartialEq / Eq — partial vs. lawful equality (library proposal 004, WI-644).
--
-- PartialEq holds the `eq`/`neq` OPERATIONS: any two-valued comparison, with NO
-- reflexivity law. This is all a body goal `eq(?x, ?y)` needs, and it is what
-- IEEE `Float` provides (`nan eq nan = false`, `-0.0 eq +0.0 = true`).
--
-- Eq `requires PartialEq` and adds ONLY the reflexivity law `eq_refl` — a lawful-
-- equivalence marker + obligation, no new operation (`Eq.eq` is the inherited
-- `PartialEq.eq`). `Set`/`Map` keys, dedup, and sort require this lawful Eq.
-- `Float` does NOT provide Eq: it cannot discharge `eq(?a,?a) <=> true` at NaN.
--
-- NB `eq_refl` is a DORMANT, documentation-only obligation: it is not discharged
-- or refuted per instance, and does not fire as a rewrite (the semantic `eq` is
-- builtin-backed). WI-658 (delivered) makes a `provides Eq[Float]` a load error
-- NOT by refuting `eq_refl` at NaN, but via the `NonEq` route below — Float
-- provides `NonEq` (witnessed non-reflexivity) and `Eq` ⊥ `NonEq` is checked at
-- load (`check_eq_noneq_exclusive`). Use-site `requires Eq[K]` enforcement — so a
-- `Map[K = Float]` field type is a load error (WI-644) — is `check_use_site_requires_eq`:
-- a parametric sort `requires Eq[param]` bound to a `NonEq` carrier is rejected.
sort anthill.prelude.PartialEq
  import anthill.kernel.{not}

  sort T = ?
  operation {
    eq(a: T, b: T) -> Bool
    neq(a: T, b: T) -> Bool
  }
  -- The law that makes `neq` DERIVED, and since WI-1125 the reason a carrier may
  -- not supply its own: `eq` is the authority, every evaluator computes `neq` as
  -- its negation (resolver `sem_eq_core`, eval `builtin_neq`, C++ `!=`), and
  -- equality dispatch keys `PartialEq.eq` suppliers alone. A `neq` binding —
  -- own member, `fact PartialEq[T = C, neq = …]`, or witness sort — was therefore
  -- accepted and honoured nowhere; it is now a load error (`CarrierSuppliesNeq`).
  -- Override `eq` instead; `neq` follows. Spec §8.3 (the rule), §8.7 (the
  -- binding forms it applies to).
  rule neq(?a, ?b) <=> not(eq(?a, ?b))
end

-- Eq: lawful (reflexive) equality. The `eq`/`neq` operations are inherited from
-- PartialEq via the requires-chain (WI-614); Eq adds only the law.
--
-- NOT DERIVED FROM `Ord`, though `Ord requires Eq` and `compare_eq` relates them
-- (WI-456): the derivation would make a SECOND `eq` supplier for the carrier, and
-- equality is dispatched by the UNIFIER, which has no bracket to select at — so
-- `AmbiguousEqDispatch` refuses it. The predicate, and the `PartialOrd` case that does
-- pass it, are stated at `ordered.anthill`'s `requires Eq[T]`; 058 §3.7/§3.8.
sort anthill.prelude.Eq
  sort T = ?

  -- `provides`, NOT `requires` (WI-1110), and the two earlier readings of this line
  -- were both wrong in a way worth keeping, because each was refuted by a measurement.
  --
  -- WI-1109 measured two variants and concluded they were mutually exclusive:
  --
  --   requires + provides  -> 1867 of 2849 tests fail, `construction is cyclic:
  --                           PartialEq[X] -> PartialEq[X]` — because the `provides`
  --                           row was filed as a PROVIDER of `PartialEq`, so `Eq`
  --                           joined the providers of the floor and `requires
  --                           PartialEq` closed the loop over it.
  --   provides alone       -> 32 fail, every one `unresolved import
  --                           'anthill.prelude.Eq.eq'` — because a `provides` clause
  --                           put nothing in this spec's SCOPE.
  --
  -- BOTH FAILURES WERE THE SAME DEFECT, and it was not in this file. A spec's
  -- `provides` is a CONVERSION — "hold an `Eq[T]` and you can obtain a `PartialEq[T]`"
  -- — so it belongs in the chain, exactly where `requires` puts its entry, and NOT in
  -- the table of what has which type. Filed correctly it brings the scope with it and it
  -- is never offered as a provider, so there is no loop left to close. The clauses were
  -- never exclusive; one of them was misfiled.
  --
  -- `Eq.eq` RESOLVES TO THE INHERITED `PartialEq.eq` IN BOTH SPELLINGS, which is what
  -- LIBRARY proposal 004 asked for ("keep `Eq.eq` resolving to the inherited
  -- `PartialEq.eq` … so most call sites are source-compatible", its migration step 1 —
  -- the tree has two 004s and this is not `docs/proposals/004-tuple-sorts.md`).
  -- THIS COMMENT USED TO CLAIM IT FLATLY AND WAS HALF TRUE: the chain served the member
  -- IMPORT (`import anthill.prelude.Eq.{eq}`), while the written-out address
  -- `Eq.eq(a, b)` was "unknown functor" — that reading is a `by_qualified_name` string
  -- join, and `anthill.prelude.Eq.eq` is not a key. WI-20260825-X9RRN gave the join a
  -- rung that follows `provides`; spec §8.6, `load::dotted_by_provision`.
  --
  -- WHAT IT BUYS over `requires`: a carrier writing `provides Eq[X]` thereby provides
  -- `PartialEq[X]` — one `eq`, one equality — instead of writing both rows and being
  -- trusted to keep them consistent (058 §3.8, and the same argument as
  -- `Ord provides WeakOrd`).
  provides PartialEq[T = T]

  -- Reflexivity — the law a partial carrier (IEEE Float) cannot satisfy. Dormant /
  -- documentation-only (see the file header): NOT discharged per instance. A
  -- `provides Eq[Float]` is blocked instead by the `NonEq` exclusion (WI-658).
  rule eq_refl: eq(?a, ?a) <=> true
end

-- NonEq: a carrier declares its equality is NOT reflexive by exhibiting a
-- WITNESS — `nonEqRefl()` returns some value `w` with `eq(w, w) = false`. This
-- is the constructive form of `∃x. eq(x, x) = false` (Anthill has no `∃` in rule
-- bodies, so the existential is Skolemized to a nullary witness operation). It
-- is what a partial carrier like IEEE `Float` provides INSTEAD of the lawful
-- `Eq`: Float cannot be reflexive (`eq(nan, nan) = false`), and says so by
-- exhibiting `nan`.
--
-- `Eq` and `NonEq` are MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE: a carrier's `eq` cannot be both
-- reflexive and non-reflexive. A load-time check (WI-658) rejects any carrier
-- that provides BOTH. A carrier that provides NEITHER is unconstrained — the
-- check does nothing without a declaration. Composite carriers (entities /
-- named tuples with a NonEq field) should derive NonEq congruently — that is a
-- follow-up (the composite Eq/NonEq derivation ticket).
sort anthill.prelude.NonEq
  sort T = ?
  requires PartialEq[T]

  -- A witness value not equal to itself. For a genuine partial carrier
  -- `eq(nonEqRefl(), nonEqRefl()) = false` (Float: `nonEqRefl() = nan`).
  operation nonEqRefl() -> T
end
